      "published_response_sha256": "f23bb876b6633efb96a92288cd224b5cad4d5646fef55f7e074b48c827ee35e5",
      "answer_text_sha256": "e89a95c86f59986c085ee2a80641b489190bc288a5c68a56ee45d115038aa6da",
      "answer_text": "The light-dependent reactions convert light energy into chemical energy stored in energy carriers, and these carriers transport that energy to the Calvin cycle [ev_008] [ev_005] [ev_007]. The light-dependent reactions use light to temporarily store energy in energy carriers [ev_005] [ev_008]. Photosystems absorb light and use electron transport chains to convert energy into the chemical energy of ATP and NADPH [ev_002]. These energy-carrying molecules travel into the stroma, where the Calvin cycle reactions take place [ev_007]. The Calvin cycle then uses the energy in ATP and NADPH to convert CO2-derived molecules into G3P [ev_010]. The energy carriers are described as \"full\" when they carry energy from the light-dependent reactions to the Calvin cycle, and as \"empty\" after the energy is released, when they return to the light-dependent reactions to obtain more energy [ev_001]. The Calvin cycle is the set of reactions that use the energy stored by the light-dependent reactions to form glucose and other carbohydrate molecules [ev_011].",
